// fx sqrt -- exact integer square root, bit by bit.
//
// There is no sqrt builtin and float is unusable (research/id-patterns.md: every
// graphics demo in the reference tree is strictly integer), so this is the
// digit-by-digit method in base 4: try to set one bit of the result at a time,
// highest first, keeping the remaining part of the radicand. It is exact -- the
// floor of the true root for every a in 0..2^31-1 -- and it never overflows,
// because the largest quantity it forms is result+bit, which peaks at
// 1342177280 (verified by exhaustive simulation) well inside int range.
//
// Newton's method would be shorter but needs a division per step and a starting
// guess, and its final "did I land one below?" fixup is the same amount of code
// as this whole loop. A negative a returns 0 rather than aborting, because a
// squared length that went negative through wraparound must not kill the frame.
//
// The running state is a 2-slot list `sq`: 0 = the part of a not yet accounted
// for, 1 = the root built so far (doubled, which is what the base-4 recurrence
// carries). A list is used rather than three threaded parameters so the caller
// keeps its own action budget.

fx_sqrt(int a) {
  int[] sq = [a, 0];
  int m = 0;
  if (a > 0) {
    m = fx_sqloop(sq, fx_sqbit(a));
  }
} return int m;

// The highest power of 4 that is not greater than a. 1073741824 is 4^15, the
// largest that fits an int, so the walk down is at most 15 steps.
fx_sqbit(int a) {
  int bt = 1073741824;
  while (bt > a) {
    bt = bt / 4;
  }
} return int bt;

fx_sqloop(int[] sq, int bt) {
  while (bt > 0) {
    fx_sqstep(sq, bt);
    bt = bt / 4;
  }
} return int sq[1];
